<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<pretext>
    <article xml:id="midterm1">
        <section xml:id="sec-mid1">
            <title>Midterm 1</title>
            <introduction>
                <p>
                    Below you will find a practice set (with solutions) and a sample exam. 
                </p>
            </introduction>
        
            <worksheet xml:id="midterm1-practice" margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm">
            <title>Midterm 1 Practice Set (with solutions)</title>
            <introduction>
                <p>
            These exercises are intended to help you think more deeply about the important concepts on Midterm 1.
You should not expect problems on the midterm to look exactly like these.
The number of problems in this review is also not indicative of the length of the midterm.
Use them as a starting place for your review and to prompt questions.
</p>
            </introduction>

<ol>
  <li>
    <p>
      Consider the graphs of two functions, <m>f(x)</m> (red) and <m>g(x)</m> (blue).
      <m>f(x)</m>:
      <image source="red.png">
      </image>
      <m>g(x)</m>:
      <image source="blue.png">
      </image>
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Circle all that apply to the function <m>f(x)</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            proportional  linear  exponential  invertible
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Circle all that apply to the function <m>g(x)</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            proportional  linear  exponential  invertible
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Find equations for <m>f(x)</m> and <m>g(x)</m>.
            Find equations for <m>f^{-1}(x)</m> and <m>g^{-1}(x).</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Compute <m>f^{-1}(-2)</m>, <m>f(g(-3))</m>, <m>f(f(-1))</m>, <m>(f+g)(-1)</m>, and <m>(f\cdot g)(-1)</m> using the graphs only.
            Then compute them using your equations from the previous part.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The function <m>f(g(x))</m> is linear.
            What is the slope? What is the <m>y</m>-intercept?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            The function <m>g(f(x))</m> is linear.
            What is the slope? What is the <m>y</m>-intercept?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      On an alien planet they use goops and snarks to measure volume.
      One goop is equal <m>\frac{9}{7}</m> times a snark.
      Pheobe the dog has a volume of 75 snarks.
      What is her volume in goops?
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      The functions below are all transformations of “parent” functions.
      First determine the “parent” function and sketch its graph.
      Then sketch the graph of the transformed function.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>f(x) = e^{x-2}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\ell(x) = 2^{-x}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>g(x) = -x^{2} -1</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>k(x) = 0.5\sqrt{x+2}</m>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>j(x) = \sin(-x)</m>
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Consider the updating function
      <me>
        p_{t+1}=p_{t} + 0.5 ,
      </me>
      where <m>p_{t}</m> is the population of fish at time <m>t</m> and the population is measured in thousands of fish.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Identify the updating function rule, and graph the updating function.
            Label all axes.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Suppose <m>p_{0}=3.</m> Use the updating function to find <m>p_{3}</m>.
            Then find the solution function that gives the number of fish (measured in thousands) as a function of time.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Consider the updating function
    </p>

    <p>
      <me>
        m_{t+1}=3m_{t}-2.
      </me>
    </p>

    <p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Graph the updating function on the axis below.
            Label all axes.
          </p>

          <p>
            <image source="grid.png">
            </image>
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Identify an initial value for which the solution function increases.
            Identify an initial value for which the solution function decreases.
            In both cases, justify your answer using cob-webbing.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Circle all equilibrium points, and determine if each is stable or unstable.
            Use cob-webbing to justify your answer.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Consider a DTDS which models population after <m>t</m> years by the updating function and initial value
      <me>
        b_{t+1}=0.7b_{t}, b_{0}=150.
      </me>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the equation of the solution function for this system.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Express the solution function in the form <m>b_{t} = b_{0}e^{kt}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            What is the half-life of the population?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            When will the population reach <m>50</m>?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            For the solution function, determine the value of <m>\dfrac{AROC_{[1,2]}}{AROC_{[0,1]}}</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Find equations for each function below without using a phase shift.
      <image source="graphs.png">
      </image>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Equation:
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Equation:
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      A person breathes in and out <m>0.75</m>L of air each breath.
      Their lungs have <m>3</m> L of air when full.
      Let <m>s_{t}</m> denote the concentration of a certain chemical in the lungs after taking <m>t</m> breaths.
      The outside concentration of the chemical being measured is <m>2</m> mmol/L.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Write an updating function for this DTDS.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Find the equilibrium point of this DTDS.
            Is it stable or unstable?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If <m>s_{0} = 1</m>, find the solution function for this DTDS.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Recognize each expression as a weighted average.
      Identify the weights, and fill in the blanks to make a true statement.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>.4(20) + .6(10)</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            The weight <em> </em> makes the number <em> </em> the “most important” in this expression.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>s(.5) + (1-s)(.7)</m>, <m>0 \leq s \leq 1</m>
          </p>

          <p>
            If the weight <em> </em> is large, then the number <em> </em> is the “least important” in this expression.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Consider the updating function
      <me>
        x_{t+1}= \frac{9x_{t}}{x_{t} +1}.
      </me>

      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Find the equilibrium points algebraically.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Use the updating function to create tables using appropriate initial conditions to determine if each equilibrium point is stable or unstable.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <p>
    </p>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      For each set of criteria, sketch the graph of a function <m>f(x)</m> that meets all criteria.
    </p>

    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                The domain of <m>f(x)</m> is <m>[-1,2]</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>f(x)</m> is invertible.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>f(0)=1</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>f(2) &#x3C; f(-1)</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>f(0)</m> is undefined (i.e., <m>0</m> is not in the domain of <m>f(x)</m>)
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                The range of <m>f(x)</m> is <m>[-1,2]</m>.
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>f(x)</m> is not invertible.
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>AROC_{[0,2]}>0</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>f(0) = -1</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>\dfrac{f(3) - f(1)}{3-1}&#x3C;0</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          <ul>
            <li>
              <p>
                <m>AROC_{[-1,1]}=0</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>AROC_{[0,1]}>0</m>
              </p>
            </li>

            <li>
              <p>
                <m>AROC_{[0,1]}&#x3C; AROC_{[1,2]}</m>
              </p>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </li>

  <li>
    <p>
      Examples of the following descriptions do not exist.
      Explain why.
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            An angle <m>\theta</m> such that <m>\tan(\theta) = \cos(\theta) = 0</m>.
            (You should reference what these quantities represent in the unit circle in your explanation.)
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            An exponential function <m>g(x) = b^{x}</m> such that <m>\dfrac{AROC_{[1,2]}}{AROC_{[0,1]}}> b</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>
  </li>
</ol>


            </worksheet>

            <worksheet xml:id="midterm1-sample" margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm">
            <title>Sample Midterm 1</title>
            

            </worksheet>
        </section>
    </article>
</pretext>